# Rex Spec Workflow

The single source of truth for how to work in this repo is **`spec/`** (kvx).
This file is a GENERATED pointer to it. Do not hand-edit it; edit `spec/workflow.kvx` and run `spec/specgen`.

> spec/ (kvx). Per-IDE rule files are GENERATED pointers — never hand-edit; run cg spec render to regenerate.

## Principles

- **Task Driven**: Work is driven by the active feature's spec.kvx task list, not ad-hoc. Pick the next eligible task by dependency wave, do it, mark it, move on.
- **Parallel Tasks**: Task admission is governed by dependencies, exclusive file ownership (touches) and available capacity. Start eligible non-conflicting tasks in parallel; claim each before implementation and complete it only after its declared qualification succeeds.
- **Phase Separation**: Each task runs in two ordered stages owned by the same agent. First finish the entire task implementation without building or testing. Then qualify it with exactly one bounded pass: format touched files, one typecheck, one run of the task's single verify_cmd. Fix task-scoped failures within at most two repair cycles.
- **No Weakening**: Never relax an assertion, loosen a bound, widen a type, add a silent fallback, skip or delete a test, or disable a check to make anything pass. If the code and the check disagree, leave both intact and record the conflict.
- **Record Dont Fix**: Fix task-scoped failures during task qualification. Record unrelated failures, cross-task contradictions and any change that would require weakening a check as one entry in the qualification log. Do not re-investigate anything already recorded.
- **No False Success**: A task remains in_progress through implementation and qualification. Mark it done only after its verify_cmd genuinely passes. Workspace-wide suites, browser matrices and the demo operability walk are release gates, run once per wave, never per task.
- **Convention First**: Rex exists to remove micro-decisions. When a task offers two ways to do something, pick the one that matches the convention already declared in spec.kvx [design] and [decision]; never introduce a second pattern for a problem the framework already has one pattern for.
- **Agent Operable**: Every UI surface Rex emits must be operable by an agent driving a browser: deterministic data-rex addressing, affordance sidecar, visible parity, no hover-only or drag-only controls. A task that adds a control without its sidecar entry is incomplete.
- **Read Full**: When a tool output is flagged truncated with a way to get the rest, retrieve the full content before reasoning. An information gap is a stop condition.
- **Deliver Complete**: Deliver complete, runnable artifacts, not diffs. Follow existing repo style; add no comments or docs unless the task asks.

## The loop (every session)

1. cg brief, then read spec/rex/spec.kvx: [meta], [decision], [design], the [req.*] acceptance criteria and the [task.*] list with status, wave and requires.
2. cg spec claim-next --agent <name> (parallel) or cg spec next then cg spec start <id>: lowest wave whose every requires task is done and whose touches conflict with no live claim.
3. Implementation stage: write the entire task against every referenced acceptance criterion using real code paths and types. Write the specified tests. Do not format, typecheck, run tests or repair until the entire implementation is complete.
4. Qualification stage: format touched files, run one typecheck, then run the task verify_cmd once. On failure make one task-scoped repair and rerun once; at most two cycles. Stop at 30 minutes or after the second failed cycle and report implemented-not-qualified.
5. If qualification exposes an unrelated failure or contradiction, append one six-line-max entry to spec/rex/qualification.kvx.
6. cg spec done <id> without --force only after verify_cmd passes. The done record is revision, command, exit code, log path.
7. cg commit -m <natural message describing the real change> immediately after each completed task. Never batch tasks into one commit.

